<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ara Pacis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13–9 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Ara Pacis Augustae, Altar of Augustan Peace</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ara Pacis Augustae, the Altar of Augustan Peace, is an altar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> dedicated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pax-romana">Pax Romana</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a> commissioned it in 13 BCE to honour the return of Augustus from three years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, and it was consecrated in 9 BCE in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campus-martius">Campus Martius</a>. Its marble friezes show sacrifices, plant life evoking abundance and a procession that includes the imperial household, supporting Augustus's regime and the claims of his dynasty. Buried under silt, it was recovered in fragments from 1568 onward and reassembled in 1938, in what is now the Museum of the Ara Pacis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ara-pacis.jpg" alt="Ara Pacis" width="330" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rabax63 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ara_Pacis_(SW).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
